<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Feb 20, 2023">
<CPscript title="Circle Packings of the Torus" >
<description>This script illustrates the rudiments of circle packing for triangulations of the torus.</description>
<text> In this script you create and display a circle packing for a triangulation of a topological torus. There are 32 circles, laid out to form a fundamental domain. The side pairings (generically there are 3, as in this example) are indicated with the color coding. 

You may need to think through this script quite carefully. Why, for example, does the display show far more than 32 circles? Try the usual display command 'disp -w -c' to see the 32. The others are known as "ghost" circles. The command 'disp -w -c -R a' includes the side pairings, and this may help you work through an explanation of the layout process.

It may also help to read up a little on the torus; a good resource is Jones and Singerman, "Complex Functions: An Algebraic and Geometric Viewpoint". The "conformal modulus' of the torus on which the packing resides is uniquely determined by the triangulation. The 'torus_tau' command displays the modulus (a complex number) in the "Message" window. </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="centrejust.png">act 0;Cleanse;Map -x;infile_read firstTor.p;max_pack;layout;set_screen -a;scale .6;set_brush 2;color -c 80 a;Disp -w -C -cf -Rt6 a;copy 1; </cmd>
<text> The side-pairing maps may be applied using the 'pair_mod' command or the icons in the window opened with the "Mobius" button. For convenience, the 3 side-pair maps and their inverses are accessible using buttons 'a', 'A', 'b', 'B', and 'c', 'C'. After pressing one of these, 'd' will display the fundamental region in its new location --- note how the side colors match up! (You may need to zoom out to see more territory. Also, you may need a "layout" command to recompute the side-pairings if you loose the domain or should the data be corrupted.) It can't hurt to give it a try.

As a backup, in case you had trouble, this next optional command will lay out surrounding copies of the fundamental domain for you; start with a 'layout' command first. </text>
<cmd name="p" mnemonic="p" iconname="idea.png">disp -w;[a];[b];[A];[b];[A];[B];[B];[B];[a];[B];[a];[b];[b];[A]; </cmd>
<text> The next command shows the same torus, but with different side pariings. In this case, there are just two side-pairings. Moreover, the torus has been put in what is generally considered "standard" normalization. Namely, one of the four corners (all associated with the save vertex) is at the origin and the next is at z=1. From this you can read off the modulus of the torus, which is given in the Message window. </text>
<cmd iconname="bookmark_add.png">act 1;torus_t;set_screen -a;set_brush 2;color -c 80 a;Disp -w -C -Rt6 a; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="d" mnemonic="d" iconname="apollon.png">disp -C -R </cmd>
<Section title="Side-pairing Mobius Transformations">
<cmd inline="no" name="a" mnemonic="a" iconname="mozilla.png">pair_mob a;[d]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="b" mnemonic="b" iconname="netbeans.png">pair_mob b;[d]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="c" mnemonic="c" iconname="klines.png">pair_mob c;[d]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="A" mnemonic="A" iconname="kghostview.png">pair_mob A;[d]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="B" mnemonic="B" iconname="amarok.png">pair_mob B;[d]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="C" mnemonic="C" iconname="apollon.png">pair_mob C;[d] </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
